ou look at a more recent  report from german in may he changed his  mind and he now believes that it'll look  like a smaller version of the current  mac pro design which to me makes the  most sense because the current design of  the mac pro is now iconic so here's an  image of what i expected to look like  being around 33 smaller than the current  model now you might be wondering how  apple is gonna have enough room for all  of the components if they're making it  that much smaller well if you think  about it there are four different things  that take up the most amount of space  inside of the current mac pro number one  is the two massive mpx modules for  graphics number two is the huge cpu  heatsink for that crazy power hungry 28  core xeon cpu  number three is the large and thick  triple fan system that covers the entire  face of the front of the mac pro and  number four is the pretty hefty 1.4  kilowatt power supply so to tackle  number one first i fully believe that  the new mac pro will not support the  current mpx modules because i can 100  guarantee you that third-party gpus like  the ones from amd will not be supported  on the new mac pro or any apple silicon  mac ever so with that said the new mac  pro will likely only have four or six  pci express slots so that professionals  can add in things like audio interface  cards io cards blackmagic deck link  cards and potentially custom apple gpu  cards if apple decides to make them but  even if they do they will no longer be  double wide cards like the current mpx  slots they'll probably only take up a  single slot just like the current  afterburner card now on to number two  that massive cpu heatsink may not need  to be that large anymore because as we  all know apple's chips are extremely  power efficient and that same reasoning  applies to the huge triple fan system  which i believe can be easily slimmed  down on the new mac pro and finally for  number four we have the absolutely  overkill 1.4 kilowatt power supply that  we will no longer need and i know this  for a fact because the highest package  power reported on the new m1 max macbook  pro has been 92 watts total and keep in  mind that's with andre from inentech  putting as much load as possible on the  chip including the cpu and the gpu at  the same time so if you multiply that by  four assuming the mac pro will have four  of these m1 max dies that brings the  power usage up to  368 watts and that's for the  top-of-the-line chat now keep in mind  that our 12 core xeon mac pro which is  slower than the 30 watt cpu in the m1  pro chip was able to use up to 218 watts  by itself let me repeat that that's just  the xeon cpu alone the 92 watt total  package power in the m1 max also  includes the gpu the neural engine the  media encoders the thunderbolt display  engines the amx co-processors the memory  buses the unified ram all of the  different caches and literally  everything else but then taking it even  further with the current mac pro the  highest nw 6900x gpu uses around 300 to  350 watts alone and then the  w6800x duo likely uses up to 500 watts  total that's more than the theoretical  maximum of 368 watts for the four m1 max  dies and that's including the cpu and  everything else so the point that i'm  making is that apple no longer needs a  crazy large power supply especially  since they won't support amd graphics  cards anymore so with that design stuff  out of the way i want to get into how i  can see apple pulling off this new 40  core m1 max quadro chip which is a name  that many other people on twitter can  agree on as we already know apple is  using a binning process to improve  yields for their chips as you can see in  this analysis by frederick orange on  twitter so for example if there are too  many defects on the bottom m1 max  portion of the die apple literally  slices off the bottom and uses it as an  m1 pro chip or if there are up to two  defective performance cores on the top  apple sells it as an 8 core base m1 pro  chip in the base 14 inch model and even  better apple secretly printed a second  neural engine on the bottom m1 max  portion just in case the one on the top  is defective to then sell it as an m1  max ship to improve yields which is  genius so my theory is that over the  next year apple is going to be  collecting perfect m1 max dies with no  flaws at all so they can be put together  in the mac pro by creating that m1 max  quadro chip by using a larger io die for  connecting them in some sort of infinity  fabric to minimize efficiency loss and  the cool thing about using perfect m1  max dies is that apple can technically  enable the second 16 core neural engine  within each die giving us up to an  insane  128 core neural engine across all of the  four m1 max dies if they really wanted  to but to make things even better the  new m1 max quadro chip will technically  have eight video encoders and decoders  as well as eight pro res encoders in  decoders so for example matthew moniz  made a video where he compared the m1  max macbook pro to a custom built  desktop pc with the brand new i9 12 900k  alder lake cpu and an rtx 3080 desktop  gpu and in his davinci resolve export  test the macbook pro smoked the desktop  pc by finishing in just a minute and 59  seconds but with eight video encoders  and decoders the m1 max quadro and the  future mac pro would finish the export  in only around 30 seconds basically  being six times faster than this rtx  3080 desktop pc which basically will  create the world's fastest computer for  video editing by far but what really  blew my mind is a programming test that  matthew ran which was compiling mozilla  firefox apparently the 10 core m1 max  chip only took one minute longer than  the alder lake 12900k which is  absolutely mind-blowing because that cpu  is now the world's fastest non-server  consumer cpu in terms of raw performance  being over two times faster than the m1  max but in this compiled test the m1 max  is barely slower because of the  efficient unified memory architecture in  fact in our xcode programming test the  10 core m1 max was only 4 seconds slower  than the insanely expensive 28 core xeon  in the current mac pro so imagine how  fast the 40 core m1 max quadro will be  in a future mac pro and just to use some  quick math the 40 core cpu could score  up to 49 348 points in cinebench r23 if  it scaled linearly but just in case i'm  going to take off 10 for efficiency loss  giving us a score of 44  400 points basically creating the  world's fastest non-server grade cpu  competing with some of the highest end  amd threadripper cpus out there but  going even further the crazy thing is  that the new m1 max quadro chip will  also have eight amx chips for matrix  multiplication it's going to have a 128  channel memory for overkill photo  editing performance and it should have  16 thunderbolt 4 controllers to support  up to a theoretical total of 12 6k  displays and 4 4k displays using hdmi  and then of course you'll have up to 256  gigs of unified memory and 128  powerful gpu cores so if you're curious  about how that crazy gpu would perform  we can actually get a rough estimate if  we take a look at the performance of the  m1 max in the optimized 3d mark wildlife  extreme benchmark the 32 core m1 max  scored almost exactly four times higher  than the eight core gpu in the m1  showing that apple achieved perfect four  time scaling in optimized workflows so  if we assume perfect four time scaling  going from a single 32 core m1 max die  up to 128 cores in an m1 max quadro chip  that would bring the score up to over 80  000 which is absolutely insane but just  to be realistic let's factor in 10  efficiency loss due to the fact that the  four dies will have to communicate over  some form of infinity fabric that brings  us down to around 73 000 points which is  actually higher than the best score ever  recorded in this 3dmark wildlife  benchmark which is 61973  using an i9 109900k and an rtx 3090 gpu  however that gpu was overclocked like  crazy with the clock speed sitting at  2.8 gigahertz compared to the base 1.4  gig frequency as well as slightly  overclocked memory speed as well but if  we adjust the boost clock speeds down to  a more realistic 1.8 gigahertz that most  of these 3090s ship with we see scores  of around 45 000 points compared to an  estimated 73 000 for the future mac pro  so the point that i'm making is that  even the integrated 128 core gpu and  this new m1 max quadro mac pro would be  the fastest consumer gpu in the world  and that's not even looking into  performance per watt metrics so based on  that i honestly don't think that apple  needs to create custom apple gpu add-in  cards but if for some reason they do  they can potentially reuse m1 max dies  that had too many flaws in other parts  of the die and simply use them to create  custom gpu chips with disabled cpu cores  but with all of that out of the way the  last point that i want to make is that a  fully loaded new mac pro will be less  expensive than the current ones so  getting into it the reason that the  current 28 core xeon cpu in the mac pro  is insanely expensive is because it  features a massive monolithic dye that  has low yields because those huge dies  take up a larger portion of the silicon  wafer so you're wasting more of the  edges of the wafer and losing dies due  to flaws but if you look at something  like apple's m1 ship the price per die  is much cheaper because they're much  smaller estimated by lacuza on twitter  to be 120 square millimeters so there's  a lot less wasted wafer space so fredrik  orange on twitter made an estimate of  the price per die and it comes out to  200 per m1 max die based on a 60 yield  rate because this is a newly designed  chip bringing the total up to 800  for four of these dies which is likely  much lower than one of those monolithic  28 core xeon dies which doesn't even  include all of the other components like  the crazy 128 core gpu and everything  else so because apple is going to be  combining multiple m1 max dies they're  saving a ton of cash compared to using  monolithic dies which means that the  price that we as consumers have to pay  will drop by a lot but with that said  let's finally get into what i expect  apple to pack into the base model as  well as the pricing and the release date  mark german believes that you'll have  two main chip options for the mac pro  the 20 core model which i think will be  called the m1 max duo and the 40 core  model or the m1 max quadro so for the  base model i expect it to come with a 20  core cpu a bend 48 core gpu that can be  upgraded to 64 cores 64 gigs of unified  memory that can be upgraded to 128 gigs  and a one terabyte ssd that can be  upgraded up to 16 terabytes and  potentially optional dedicated  traditional ram if they choose to add  that in for specific workloads and as  for my estimates for the base price i  think it'll start anywhere from four  thousand to six thousand dollars and  then upgrading from the base chip to the  top of the line 40 core chip with 128  gpu cores and 256 gigs of ram will cost  somewhere around 8 000  for that crazy upgrade which is  significantly cheaper than the cpu and  gpu upgrades on the current mac pro and  then of course you've got to add in  storage upgrades and potentially  traditional ram upgrades and maybe even  add in custom gpu cards if apple decides  to go that route as well but of course  this is all just speculation but one  thing i do know for sure is that the new  mac pro will likely go up for sale in  either november or december of next year  just in time for business right off  season so there you guys go hopefully i  was able to put some things into  perspective and i want to mention that i  fully believe that this apple silicon  mac pro will be the fastest consumer  available computer in the world  by far for both professional work and  gaming basically giving you a super  computer at home so hopefully you  enjoyed this video and if you did go  ahead and click the circle about to  subscribe definitely check out one of  those two videos right over there thanks  for watching and we'll see you in the  next video  [Music]

